A||||7||Fiero Sam||Engine Swap||||5||
Z||000000||Fiero Sam||07-18-2001||05:40 PM||||My Fiero is a 1987 automatic 4 cylinder coupe with 143,000 miles.The motor runs and sounds really good.A guy at work(not sure how much he knows about cars)says I will be needing to replace the engine soon.Should I be thinking about this if the engine sounds and runs good?Will an engine give any indication that it is about to die,or will it just go?If I should be thinking about a new engine,what size/type would you recommend?I am not looking to turn it into a street racer or anything.I intend to keep this car as long as possible(if I live long enough to put 12 motors in it,I will).I mostly drive to work in back and have fun in it.I am not looking to impress anyone(except myself).Any advice would be appreciated(even if I spelled the word wrong).<BR>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||jake1852||07-18-2001||06:12 PM||||if the engine runs well, and still has its power......i dont see the need to replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||07-18-2001||07:43 PM||||I know many people who have over 200000 on their V6 Fiero and some have 200-400000 miles on their 2.5 duke.  After 150000, I'd keep an eye open and an ear to the the car's engine.  Set aside some $ for the engine swap of the future.  When the engine needs to be replaced, probably many other things will need to be replaced as well.  If you go for new you'd looking at $4000 for a "professional" replacement.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-18-2001||07:48 PM||||These engines will last a long time if taken care of. Problem is most people don't take care of any thing on a car let alone the engine.<P>Keep the oil changes up is probably most important. As long as you don't have any oil leaks, stuff Mobil1 in it. I started putting it in mine at over 150,000. I won't go back to crude unless I'm really strapped for cash. <P>Next, if it's been more than a couple years, do a flush and new coolant. That will protect the whole cooling system from problems.<P>If you are that worried about it.... these motors are easy to find. GM used the same motor in many FWD cars thru 91. They are plentiful and cheap. Find a good one and crate it up for future use.<P>Asside from simply wearing it out, the most likely major thing that could up and fail w/o warning would be the cam gear. That isn't very hard to fix, just time consuming as hell.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||07-19-2001||02:12 AM||||just like theogre says, if you take good and proper care of it the engine should last quite a while. There is an 85 coupe here in the shop (4-cyl) with over 300,000 miles on it and it is being restored (with the original engine) for the owner......||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||07-19-2001||04:57 PM||||I got a 88 engine thats needs to be 'crated' up so it can be stored. :/ Do the newer 89-91 dukes change any from the 88 model? in performance #'s or whatnot?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero Sam||07-19-2001||05:49 PM||||Thanks for the information.I appreciate everyone's input.There are alot of good ideas around here.<BR>I feel alot better about it now.I wasn't so much worried,I just want to be prepared if I needed to be.I don't want to get sold something I don't want.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||07-19-2001||07:31 PM||||As long as the compression checks good, oil consumption is minimal, and there is no internal noise (knocking and such) the engine is fine and should be left alone.  There is no standard for engine life.  The most important factor is proper care.  Save your pennies for that rainiy day.  It may come tomorrow or in ten years.  But you engine is most likely perfectly fine.||||reg||4
